Association & Multiplilicities:
Patient 1..* Appointment
Practitioner 1..* Appointment
Appointment links exactly one patient and one practitioner

E - AI Design Review: AI ON
AI was asked to propose classes only from confirmed requirements and cite requirement IDs.

AI Suggestions
- Add UserRole class → supported by NFR-03 (security)
- Add ScheduleView class → supported by FR-09
- Add AppointmentManager → unsupported (implementation detail)
- Add NotificationManager → unsupported (not in requirements)

F - Compare & Decide:
Accepted:
- UserRole
Reason: NFR-03 requires secure access control.

Modified:
- ScheduleView → integrated into Practitioner instead of separate class.
Reason: FR-09 supports schedule viewing but not a new class.

Rejected:
- AppointmentManager, NotificationManager, ClinicController
Reason: No supporting requirement IDs; these are implementation details.

G - Python Skeltons:
Patient - 
class Patient:
    def __init__(self, patient_id, name, contact):
        self.patient_id = patient_id
        self.name = name
        self.contact = contact
        self.history = []

Practioner - 
class Practitioner:
    def __init__(self, practitioner_id, name, availability):
        self.practitioner_id = practitioner_id
        self.name = name
        self.availability = availability
        self.schedule = []

Appointments - 
class Appointment:
    def __init__(self, appointment_id, date, time, patient, practitioner, status):
        self.appointment_id = appointment_id
        self.date = date
        self.time = time
        self.patient = patient
        self.practitioner = practitioner
        self.status = status

H — Consistency Check
- All classes match the domain model.
- Attributes in Python match UML attributes.
- Associations represented through object references.
- No extra behaviour implemented (as required).
- No technical classes (managers, controllers) included.

Reflection:
The hardest modelling decision was determing which concepts belonged in the domain model versus which were implementation
details. For example, appointment manager and clinic controller sounded useful, but they did not represent real world 
entities and had no supporiting requirements. The AI frequently over designed by suggesting technical classes that belong
in software architecture, not the domain model. The evidence that guided my final chocies came directly from the functional 
requirements; each domain class had to map to an observable capability. Patient, practioner, and appointment clearly
aligned with FR-01 through FR-10, while concepts like notification manger had no reuqirement support and were rejected.
Another challenge was  deciding whether schedule view should be a class. The AI suggested it with evidence from FR-09,
but the reuqirement only described a behaviour, not a new eneity, so I modeified the suggestion and kept sechdule viewing
inside the practioner class. Overall, evidence from the requirements ensured the model stayed simple, accurate, and aligned 
with the small clinic scope.





